<body>

  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to main content</a>

  <header>
    <h1>🐱 Cat FAQ</h1>
    <p>Everything you wanted to know about our feline friends.</p>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <p class="section-label">Frequently Asked Questions</p>

    <div class="faq-list">

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Why do cats purr?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>Cats purr by rapidly dilating and constricting muscles in their larynx, which modulates airflow during both inhale and exhale — creating that signature rumble.</p>
          <p>While contentment is the most common trigger, cats also purr when stressed, injured, or giving birth. The 25–150 Hz frequency range is even thought to promote bone healing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Why do cats knock things off tables?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>It's a mix of hunting instinct and sensory curiosity. Cats use their paws to probe objects the way they'd test potential prey — checking if it will move or flee.</p>
          <p>There's also a social dimension: knocking something over immediately gets your attention, which cats learn to exploit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            How many hours a day do cats sleep?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>On average, 12–16 hours per day — and senior or very young cats can sleep up to 20 hours. As obligate carnivores, cats evolved to conserve energy between short, intense bursts of hunting.</p>
          <p>Most of that sleep is light dozing; deep REM sleep accounts for only about 25% of their total rest.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Do cats always land on their feet?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>Usually, yes — thanks to their <em>righting reflex</em>. A flexible spine and lack of a functional collarbone let cats rotate their body mid-air to orient feet-down, even from very short drops.</p>
          <p>However, falls from very low heights (under ~1–2 feet) don't give them enough time to right themselves, and high falls can still cause injuries despite the reflex.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Why do cats bring dead animals as gifts?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>Mother cats bring prey back to their kittens to teach hunting. Indoor cats may see their human as an incompetent hunter who needs feeding — or simply as part of their social group to share with.</p>
          <p>It's ultimately a sign of trust and affection, even if the delivery method is… unwelcome.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Can cats see in complete darkness?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>Not in <em>complete</em> darkness — they still need at least some light. But they need roughly six times less light than humans do. Their eyes have a high density of rod cells and a reflective layer (the <em>tapetum lucidum</em>) that bounces light back through the retina for a second pass.</p>
          <p>That mirror layer is also why their eyes appear to glow in photos or at night.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h2>
          <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="true">
            Why do cats knead with their paws?
            <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </h2>
        <div class="details">
          <p>Kneading starts in kittenhood as a way to stimulate milk flow from the mother. Adult cats retain the behaviour as a soothing, comfort-seeking action — often a sign they feel safe and relaxed.</p>
          <p>Scent glands in their paw pads also mean they're subtly marking the surface (and you) as belonging to them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div><!-- /.faq-list -->
  </main>

  <script>
